```rust
/// One node a levelled walk reached, at the first level whose pass reached it.
#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
#[non_exhaustive]
pub struct LevelReached { pub id: NodeId, pub level: u8, pub depth: usize, pub via: EdgeCategoryId, pub parent: NodeId,
    /// Position of the reaching edge among the index's kept edges (global edge order filtered by the kept categories).
    pub kept_edge: u32 }
#[derive(Clone, Debug, Default, PartialEq, Eq)]
#[non_exhaustive]
pub struct LevelBfs { pub reached: Vec<LevelReached> /* level ascending, discovery order within a level */, pub seeds: Vec<(NodeId, u8)> /* indexed seeds, each at its lowest given level, input order */, pub scanned: usize }
#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug)]
#[non_exhaustive]
pub struct LevelWalk<'a> { pub walk: Walk, pub levels: u8, pub max_depth: usize,
    /// Dense-index mask (len == adj.len()): a node outside is never entered or reported.
    pub within: Option<&'a [bool]>,
    /// Dense-index rank: each depth layer is expanded in ascending (rank, dense index) order.
    pub parent_rank: Option<&'a [u32]>,
    /// The transitive-closure `+`: seeds start the walk unvisited, so a seed is reported when one or more hops reach it (itself, on a cycle).
    pub plus: bool }
impl<'a> LevelWalk<'a> { pub fn new(walk: Walk, levels: u8) -> Self /* max_depth usize::MAX, no mask, no rank, plus false */ }
pub fn bfs_levels(adj: &Adjacency, seeds: &[(NodeId, u8)], edge_level: &[u8], how: &LevelWalk<'_>) -> LevelBfs;
```
Semantics. Pass L runs for L in 0..how.levels. In pass L every seed whose level <= L starts at depth 0 (a seed the index does not hold, or outside the mask, is skipped), and the incidence at kept position k is walked only when edge_level[k] <= L (u8::MAX therefore means never). Pass L is a plain BFS (visited reset per pass, O(V+E)); a node first reached in pass L is pushed to `reached` with level L and that pass's depth / via / parent / kept_edge; a node already reported in an earlier pass is walked again but not re-reported, so every node appears once at its smallest level and `reached` is level-ascending. A seed appears in `seeds` at its smallest given level and (plus = false) in `reached` only when an earlier pass reached it first. With plus = true seeds are enqueued at depth 0 WITHOUT being marked visited, so a seed is reported (once, at the first level) when a walk of >= 1 hop reaches it - from another seed or from itself around a cycle - which is the transitive closure `+` the rules layer's reaches(x, y) means (DA.11); a seed reached that way is expanded a second time, which finds nothing new, so the pass stays O(V+E). `max_depth` as in `bfs` (a node at depth >= max_depth is reached, not expanded). Without `parent_rank` each pass visits in queue order, so `levels = 1` with every edge_level 0 reproduces `bfs`'s reached sequence exactly (same ids, depths, vias, parents, order - a parity test pins it). With `parent_rank` each pass is layered: the depth-d frontier is sorted by (rank, dense index) before expansion, so a node's parent is the lowest-ranked node at depth d-1 with a walkable edge to it (within that parent, the first such edge in edge order), and discovery order is canonical for the caller's ranks. A witness at exactly one level L is one pass: edge_level' = if lvl <= L {0} else {u8::MAX}, seeds at 0, levels = 1. Guards (no panic in non-test code): edge_level.len() != adj.kept_edges(), or a mask / rank slice whose length is not adj.len(), returns an empty LevelBfs and prints `[algo] bfs_levels: <what> has <n> entries for <m>`. `scanned` counts incidences examined over all passes (at most 4 x kept x levels). Walk::Forward / Backward / Both use the same step logic as `bfs` (Both merges a node's out- and in-list by edge position, self-loop once). A node's reported parent / depth / kept_edge are those of its FIRST pass, so following reported parents can mix passes (a valid path at <= the node's level, not necessarily a shortest one at that level): a shortest path at one level is the one-level walk above, which is what glia-rules' witnesses (DA.11 path) and DB's transitive forbid use. Module doc of reach.rs gains one paragraph naming the levelled walk and its first consumer (glia-rules' closure builtins, DA.11).

